<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The meadow's climate (design 28 §5): the outdoor curve the unenclosed world reads and the
  ground's answer to it.

  EVERY TEMPERATURE IS CENTI-DEGREES — 900 is 9 °C — because the whole thermal model is integer
  and one hundredth of a degree is the resolution a pass moves a room by.

  The curve is annualMean + one offset per month + a daily term. What that gives the seasons:

    Wash  (Larkspur, Tansy)     means 15 °C and 19 °C — spring, benign by design
    Glare (Bramble, Ember)      means 22 °C and 27 °C — summer, warm afternoons, no danger
    Rime  (Hollow, Candle)      means 2 °C and −8 °C — "the one that kills you"

  With the ±5 °C daily swing, Wash nights bottom at 10 °C — the mild band, full work rate,
  sleep at nine tenths: shelter is worth wanting in spring and not yet needed. Hollow's nights
  cross below zero and Candle's sit at −13 °C: exposure, not instant death — severity fills in
  about four hours outdoors — and a cold snap's −25 °C below seasonal is the lethal
  hypothermia-within-hours the almanac already promises. Comfort is 16–26 °C, so Glare
  afternoons are merely warm and Rime is the season the campfire exists for.

  The ground damping (717 per layer = e^(−1/3), a damping depth of three 3 m layers) makes a
  cellar lag the season and a deep mine hold the annual mean — the reason digging down is worth
  doing in Rime at all.
-->
<Defs>

  <ClimateDef>
    <defName>Climate_Temperate</defName>
    <label>temperate</label>
    <annualMeanC>900</annualMeanC>
    <monthlyOffsetC>
      <li>600</li>
      <li>1000</li>
      <li>1300</li>
      <li>1800</li>
      <li>-700</li>
      <li>-1700</li>
    </monthlyOffsetC>
    <dailyAmplitudeC>500</dailyAmplitudeC>
    <groundOneLayerDampingPerMille>717</groundOneLayerDampingPerMille>
  </ClimateDef>

</Defs>
